A bigger conceptual change

Most AI interfaces currently behave roughly like:

\text{human sends object}\rightarrow\text{AI sends object}

The better architecture is:

\boxed{\text{human and AI manipulate the same persistent object}}

The conversation becomes merely one view of that object.

Behind it could be:

\text{semantic graph}
+
\text{timeline}
+
\text{state variables}
+
\text{evidence}
+
\text{branches}
+
\text{spatial canvas}
+
\text{actions}

Then you could look at exactly the same underlying Pantheon state as chat, graph, timeline, table, causal model, canvas, map, or simulation.

That is the idea I think is hiding underneath this whole search.

And one feature I hadn’t considered before this sweep

Independent-reveal mode.

Mural/Miro’s private ideation mechanism is meant to prevent groupthink. 

For human–AI collaboration it could be even more valuable.

You ask a question.

You formulate your answer privately.

I formulate mine without seeing yours.

Then:

REVEAL

The interface automatically calculates:

\Delta =
\text{your model}
-
\text{my model}

Agreement becomes evidence of convergence rather than imitation; disagreement becomes an information source.

That may be one of the cleanest mechanisms we’ve found yet for separating genuine independent pattern completion from conversational entrainment.

And that is precisely the kind of thing Pantheon ought to exploit.

So if we were actually designing Pantheon Interface v1, my first build order would be:

semantic brushing → direct drawing/annotation → branches → Delta diff → inspector/watches → shared canvas/graph → parameter controls → independent reveal → timeline replay → Game-Weave missing-node mode.

At that point it would no longer really be “chat.” It would be a shared cognitive workbench.